WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.010
Picture an assessment.

2
00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.722
The assessor asks who helps the person at

3
00:00:03.722 --> 00:00:06.004
home, and the person's mother says, family takes

4
00:00:06.004 --> 00:00:06.860
care of family.

5
00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:08.390
What gets written down?

6
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.560
Probably something like, daily help covered by family.

7
00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:13.750
It sounds clear.

8
00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:15.460
It sounds like a strong family.

9
00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:17.190
And it might be.

10
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.940
But in this case the mother works nights.

11
00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:21.899
The family didn't want to look like they

12
00:00:21.899 --> 00:00:23.509
couldn't cope in front of someone from the

13
00:00:23.509 --> 00:00:23.710
county.

14
00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:25.960
So the clear answer was true and incomplete

15
00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:26.880
at the same time.

16
00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.717
And if nobody catches that, the plan has

17
00:00:29.717 --> 00:00:31.983
a hole in it on exactly the nights

18
00:00:31.983 --> 00:00:32.550
that matter.

19
00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.080
That's where a cultural navigator can help.

20
00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:38.043
A navigator is a trusted go-between, often someone

21
00:00:38.043 --> 00:00:40.755
from the person's community, who helps a family

22
00:00:40.755 --> 00:00:42.790
and a system understand each other.

23
00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.260
In this case the navigator simply asked the

24
00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.360
mother whether she wanted to mention her schedule.

25
00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:49.810
So the navigator didn't answer for the family.

26
00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:51.440
No, and that matters.

27
00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.270
The family still speaks for itself.

28
00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.578
The navigator explains context, goes along to meetings,

29
00:00:56.578 --> 00:00:59.515
connects people to resources, follows up, and tells

30
00:00:59.515 --> 00:01:02.453
the agency where a process keeps tripping people

31
00:01:02.453 --> 00:01:02.820
up.

32
00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:05.743
Is there evidence this works, or is it

33
00:01:05.743 --> 00:01:06.890
just a nice idea?

34
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.647
There's some good evidence when navigation is tied

35
00:01:09.647 --> 00:01:10.720
to a specific step.

36
00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.687
A 2021 randomized trial of family navigation for

37
00:01:13.687 --> 00:01:16.364
young children who screened positive for autism found

38
00:01:16.364 --> 00:01:19.041
that more families got a diagnostic answer within

39
00:01:19.041 --> 00:01:19.710
a year.

40
00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.240
The difference was largest for Hispanic families.

41
00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:25.562
The authors also found results varied by clinic,

42
00:01:25.562 --> 00:01:27.370
so it's promising, not magic.

43
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.460
Here's a question I hear a lot.

44
00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:32.403
If the navigator speaks the family's language, can

45
00:01:32.403 --> 00:01:33.730
we skip the interpreter?

46
00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:35.120
Book both.

47
00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:38.190
The interpreter carries the words accurately in both

48
00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:38.510
directions.

49
00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:41.044
The navigator helps the family make sense of

50
00:01:41.044 --> 00:01:41.560
the process.

51
00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:44.490
If the navigator is interpreting, nobody is doing

52
00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:45.130
the navigating.

53
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:47.886
And the version where we ask the bilingual

54
00:01:47.886 --> 00:01:49.872
coworker to just come along on a few

55
00:01:49.872 --> 00:01:50.120
visits?

56
00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:52.690
That's navigation work without the job.

57
00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.176
It adds work nobody counts, and it can

58
00:01:55.176 --> 00:01:57.371
leave that coworker caught between their community and

59
00:01:57.371 --> 00:01:57.920
their role.

60
00:01:58.250 --> 00:01:59.912
If the need is real, treat it as

61
00:01:59.912 --> 00:02:01.574
real work, and tell someone who can fix

62
00:02:01.574 --> 00:02:01.990
the pattern.

63
00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.620
So for someone listening who isn't a navigator,

64
00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.060
what's the one thing to do?

65
00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:08.382
When an answer could mean more than one

66
00:02:08.382 --> 00:02:09.950
thing, check before you write it down.

67
00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.200
Ask about specific hours and tasks.

68
00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:14.675
And if a navigator might help, ask the

69
00:02:14.675 --> 00:02:15.990
person whether they'd like one.

70
00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:18.582
It's their choice, and what they share is

71
00:02:18.582 --> 00:02:19.460
their choice too.

72
00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:22.463
Check the answer, offer the choice, and give

73
00:02:22.463 --> 00:02:23.670
everyone a clear role.

74
00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:25.160
I can do that on Monday.
